# Python
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*.egg-info/
.eggs/

# Virtual environments / uv
.venv/
venv/

# Test & lint caches
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.coverage
coverage.xml
htmlcov/

# Test scratch: integration tests generate fixtures under the skill's plans/ dir
skills/multi-llm/plans/

# This repo's own working plans: local scratch, never pushed. Any comment below
# (or in the Makefile, .dockerignore, Dockerfile, README or sources) that cites a
# plans/*.md design doc is citing a local-only note that a fresh clone will not have.
/plans/

# Multi-LLM local config & workspace temp dir (this repo dogfoods its own skill;
# .multi-llm/tmp/ is additionally self-ignored via its own .gitignore in consuming repos)
.multi-llm/

# Multi-LLM generated artifacts (plan-local state & outputs)
# These are created next to plans at runtime and must not be committed.
**/plans/**/state.json
**/plans/**/review-plan/
**/plans/**/review-tasks/
**/plans/**/review-code/
**/plans/**/code-review/
**/plans/**/apply-suggestions/
**/plans/**/apply-task-suggestions/
**/plans/**/apply-code-fixes/
**/plans/**/implement/
**/plans/**/ask/
plan_review_output.json

# Containerized harness E2E (C6b rule 3 of the local-only plans/containerized-harness-e2e.md).
# Anchored by location, never by bare filename: `auth.json` is literally opencode's
# credential filename, so a bare `*auth.json` would also shadow the value-free seed
# templates we must commit.
#
# the raw evidence bundles
# (contents, not the directory itself: git cannot re-include a file whose parent
# directory is excluded, and the README that explains the directory stays tracked)
skills/multi-llm/tests/container/runs/*
!skills/multi-llm/tests/container/runs/README.md
# ...and the run records: every harness run writes fresh ones, so they are local
# only. The exceptions are read by something: the schema (harness.py validates
# against it), the codex release network-check records (the publish-codex-plugin
# workflow requires one on the default branch), the opencode release-gate
# records (publish-opencode-plugin.yml's `verify` job refuses without one naming
# the tag: row 8 runs only on the maintainer's machine, and this record is how
# its evidence reaches CI) and the opencode release network-check records (the
# canonical registry-install check after each npm publication, which the
# capabilities promotion and RECORDED_SMOKES cite; named exactly, so a
# `...-network-check-rehearsal.json` stays ignored). Bootstrap records stay
# workflow artifacts and match none of these.
skills/multi-llm/tests/container/records/*
!skills/multi-llm/tests/container/records/record.schema.json
!skills/multi-llm/tests/container/records/*-codex-release-network-check*.json
!skills/multi-llm/tests/container/records/*-opencode-release-gate.json
!skills/multi-llm/tests/container/records/*-opencode-release-network-check.json
**/creds/
*.credentials.json
.env
.env.*
# credential files by the path they actually live at:
**/.codex/auth.json
**/.local/share/opencode/auth.json
# ...but the value-free seed templates stay tracked:
!skills/multi-llm/tests/container/home_seed/**

# Editor / OS
.idea/
.vscode/
.zed/
.DS_Store
*.bak
*.swp

# Local Claude Code state. Named file by file and anchored at the root, never as
# a bare `.claude/`: this repo dogfoods its own skill, so `.claude/skills/` holds
# shared project tooling that must stay tracked, and a directory rule would
# shadow it. Both entries below are per-machine by construction — a permissions
# set someone accepted once, and a scheduler lock.
/.claude/settings.local.json
/.claude/scheduled_tasks.lock
# A personal workflow skill, kept out of the repo by choice. Named by its own
# directory so the rest of `.claude/skills/` stays tracked.
/.claude/skills/autopilot/

# Personal helpers that link or copy this checkout's skill into other repos on
# this machine. Anchored to the root.
/link-skill.sh
/sync-skill.sh

# Personal scratch kept beside the checkout, not a repo artifact. Anchored, so a
# `note.md` that a subdirectory ever means to track is unaffected.
/note.md
# Same rule, one directory up in size: the probe bench. Throwaway Dockerfiles,
# capture scripts and multi-hundred-megabyte build logs from working out what a
# new CLI does before its adapter exists (`scratch/aider-capture/`,
# `scratch/copilot-probe/`, the `Dockerfile.probe-*` set). The *findings* land in
# an adapter, a fixture row and a plan note; the bench they were found on is not
# a repo artifact and at 406 MB would dwarf the tree that is. Anchored to the
# root for the same reason as `/note.md`: a `scratch/` a subdirectory ever means
# to track is unaffected.
/scratch/

# The opencode npm package's build artifacts. All three are produced by
# packaging/opencode/build.py, and a staged 4.1 MB skill copy committed by
# accident is a silent second source of truth for the very tree the
# single-source rules exist to keep single.
#
# KEEP THESE HOST-ANCHORED, and never "tidy" them into `packaging/*/skills/` or
# `packaging/**`. A sibling packaging plan takes the opposite decision on
# purpose — its payload is CHECKED IN and byte-compared against a fresh build —
# so a generalised rule would silently untrack that plan's entire product.
packaging/opencode/dist/
packaging/opencode/skills/
packaging/opencode/*.tgz
# LICENSE is COPIED in by the build (never symlinked — a symlink inside an npm
# tarball is a portability hazard), so committing it would put a second copy of
# the root licence under version control, free to drift from the one that
# governs. README.md beside it is the opposite case and stays tracked: it is the
# npm landing page, authored, and not the repo README.
packaging/opencode/LICENSE

# The pre-publish gate's stamp. Safe host-agnostically: no packaging route ever
# commits one, because a stamp attests to the machine that wrote it.
packaging/*/.package-check.stamp
# ...and the per-tier evidence the codex gate writes for it (packaging/codex/release_gate.py),
# and the per-row session evidence the opencode gate writes (packaging/opencode/release_gate.py).
packaging/codex/.gate/
packaging/opencode/.gate/

# aider's repo-map cache, dropped at the root by the chain-aider container row
# when it runs against this tree. A per-machine index of the repo, not a source
# of truth about it.
.aider.tags.cache.v4/
